@joeksy Whoops, I've messed up the grep pattern. It should have been `grep -i ^x-oauth-scopes` to show the scopes that the token has, not the ones that are accepted by the endpoint. Could you run that?

As for `gh auth status`, you can run it with `DEBUG=api` which will show you the verbose log of API requests on standard error stream. You can inspect it for your Enterprise server to try and see what might have gone wrong.

From the `HTTP/2 200` response, it looks like your token is indeed valid, but I can't yet figure out why doesn't GitHub CLI want to use it. Was the error message printed from `gh auth status` just a generic "authentication failed"?

@joeksy Thanks for all your help; I finally managed to reproduce based on your setup. This is definitely a bug with `gh auth status`: it reports "You are not logged into any GitHub hosts" while you do have valid environment variables set up.

However, you should be able to use all other `gh` commands for your Enterprise instance without problem. Can you try if that works, for example listing the repos in an org your user has access to: `gh repo list <myorg>`?

I think this is only a display bug with `auth status`, not an actual authentication bug with Enterprise and your token. Thanks for your patience and help us narrow this down!
